  "query_preview": "..Strong high pressure will shift westward and build \nover Arizona today. Monsoon activity will continue to diminish \ndue to subsidence under the ridge plus drier air gradually \nworking into region. However, there remains enough moisture and \ninstability for isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms\ntoday. Coverage will be mostly restricted to the high terrain \nfrom Flagstaff southward, and will be maximized around the White \nMountains. With very slow storm motion and PW still near the 75th \npercentile, a low-end flash flood threat over burn scars remains a\nfactor. This somewhat suppressed period of monsoonal activity is \nforecast to continue through at least the weekend. Showers and \nthunderstorms will not fully shut off each day, but remain \nisolated to scattered with the best coverage in the White \nMountains.\n\nOutside of storm activity, the primary hazard over the next few days \nwill be heat. With the declination in moisture, above normal heights \nat 500mb will lead to the warmest temperatures of the year so far \nfor all of northern AZ. As a result, a variety of heat headlines \nwill be in effect. An Extreme Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon \nbelow 4000 ft will remain in effect until Sunday with daytime \ntemperatures up to 110-115 F. Additional Extreme Heat Warnings will \nbe in effect today through Sunday for Glen/Marble canyon and low\nelevation areas south of the Mogollon Rim, where temperatures \nwill approach or exceed 110 F. Valleys north of the Rim will have \na Heat Advisory on Saturday and Sunday, with daytime temperatures \nup to 100-105 F.\n\nBy the early to middle part of next week, ensemble guidance is \nleaning towards the high weakening and shifting east. This would \nallow monsoonal activity to increase again. There is still some \nuncertainty on the exact timing and nature of this transition, and \nthere is potential that heat headlines will be extended past Sunday. \nRight now the forecast calls for continued hot temperatures with \ndiminished monsoonal activity on Monday and Tuesday.",
